This is a stale closure. Your effect has an empty dependency array, so React runs it once, after the first render, and the function inside it captures the `count` from that render. When `count` was 0, the interval callback was created with 0 in scope, and it keeps logging 0 no matter how many times you update state afterwards. The state does change, but the callback never sees the new value because it is still the old function from the first render.
There are a few ways to fix it. The simplest is to put `count` in the dependency array: `}, [count]);`. React will then clear the old interval and start a new one every time `count` changes, which is correct but resets the timer on each update. If you don't want that, keep the latest value in a ref and read from it inside the interval:
```js
const countRef = useRef(count);
countRef.current = count;
useEffect(() => {
  const id = setInterval(() => console.log(countRef.current), 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(id);
}, []);
```
And if what you really want is to update state from inside the interval, use the functional form, `setCount(c => c + 1)`, which doesn't need the current value in scope at all.
The `react-hooks/exhaustive-deps` ESLint rule warns about exactly this, so it's worth turning on if you haven't. One more thing: in development, React 18 StrictMode runs effects twice on mount (setup, cleanup, setup again), so you may see doubled logs or a restarted interval. That is expected and doesn't happen in production, so don't mistake it for part of this bug.
